    13	
    14	Modern programming languages promote software reuse via package managers that facilitate the integration
    15	of inter-dependent software libraries. Software reuse comes with the challenge of dependency bloat, which
    16	refers to unneeded and excessive code incorporated into a project through reused libraries. Such bloat exhibits
    17	security risks and maintenance costs, increases storage requirements, and slows down load times. In this work,
    18	we conduct a large-scale, fine-grained analysis to understand bloated dependency code in the PyPI ecosystem.
    19	Our analysis is the first to focus on different granularity levels, including bloated dependencies, bloated files,
    20	and bloated methods. This allows us to identify the specific parts of a library that contribute to the bloat. To do
    21	so, we analyze the source code of 1,302 popular Python projects and their 3,232 transitive dependencies. For
    22	each project, we employ a state-of-the-art static analyzer and incrementally construct the fine-grained project
    23	dependency graph (FPDG), a representation that captures all inter-project dependencies at method-level.
    24	Our reachability analysis on the FPDG enables the assessment of bloated dependency code in terms of several
    25	aspects, including its prevalence in the PyPI ecosystem, its relation to software vulnerabilities, its root causes,
    26	and developer perception. Our key finding suggests that PyPI exhibits significant resource underutilization:
    27	more than 50% of dependencies are bloated. This rate gets worse when considering bloated dependency code
    28	at a more subtle level, such as bloated files and bloated methods. Our fine-grained analysis also indicates
    29	that there are numerous vulnerabilities that reside in bloated areas of utilized packages (15% of the defects
    30	existing in PyPI). Other major observations suggest that bloated code primarily stems from omissions during
    31	code refactoring processes and that developers are willing to debloat their code: Out of the 36 submitted pull
    32	requests, developers accepted and merged 30, removing a total of 35 bloated dependencies. We believe that
    33	our findings can help researchers and practitioners come up with new debloating techniques and development
    34	practices to detect and avoid bloated code, ensuring that dependency resources are utilized efficiently.
