    72	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
    73	
    74	Over the past decade, software reuse [Krueger 1992; Mohagheghi and Conradi 2007; Naur and
    75	Randell 1969] has experienced a significant rise. This trend is driven by the growing popularity of
    76	open-source software and the extensive use of package managers. Open-source software libraries
    77	are hosted in centralized repositories and are made accessible through package managers, such
    78	as Python’s `pip`, Java’s Maven, or JavaScript’s `npm` [Cox 2019; Spinellis 2012]. Developers specify
    79	project dependencies in a textual file, allowing package managers to automate the process of fetching
    80	the required library versions from the repositories [Boldi and Gousios 2020]. While software reuse
    81	offers the benefits of reduced development and maintenance costs [Mohagheghi and Conradi
    82	2007], it also introduces security and reliability risks [Cox 2019; Gkortzis et al . 2019], or license
    83	compatibility issues among dependencies [German et al. 2010; van der Burg et al. 2014].
    84	This study focuses on an emerging challenge originating from code reuse, that is, the presence
    85	of bloated dependency code. Introduced in 2021 by Soto-Valero et al . [2021b], this concept refers
    86	to the incorporation of unused code into a software project via dependencies (reused libraries).
    87	Research indicates that bloated dependencies (1) introduce dependency conflicts [Patra et al . 2018;
    88	Wang et al . 2020] and increased maintenance effort [Jafari et al . 2022; Soto-Valero et al . 2021b],
    89	(2) are related to vulnerable code [Azad et al . 2023, 2019]. To illustrate the negative effects of
    90	[bloated dependency code, consider the case of a Python project named zopefoundation/Zope. This](https://github.com/zopefoundation/Zope)
    91	project declares an unused dependency, which breaks the corresponding build process due to
    92	incompatibility issues with other declared dependencies. To fix this issue, the developers of `Zope`
    93	simply remove the bloated dependency from their codebase [Gmach 2021].
    94	In spite of its importance, bloated dependency code has only been touched by a few studies. Some
    95	of them have addressed bloated dependencies in various software ecosystems such as Maven [Ponta
    96	et al . 2021; Soto-Valero et al . 2021a] and Rust’s Cargo [Hejderup et al . 2022], with others exploring
    97	dependency issues, including bloated dependencies in Python [Cao et al . 2023] and JavaScript [Jafari
    98	et al . 2022]. Surprisingly, all those studies quantify the prevalence of bloat only at package level (e.g.,
    99	number of bloated dependencies). This can result in false estimations regarding bloated dependency
   100	code, as the actual code reuse occurs at the level of methods (e.g. a programmer might import
   101	a certain dependency, but not actually invoke any of its code) [Boldi and Gousios 2020]. This
   102	consideration demands a more fine-grained analysis for quantifying bloated dependency code.
   103	In this work, we perform the first fine-grained inter-project dependency analysis of bloated
   104	dependency code in Python projects. Python, being one of the most popular programming languages [Cosentino et al . 2017; GitHub 2023], has experienced a 24% growth in 2023 according to
   105	GitHub’s annual statistics [GitHub 2023]. Additionally, Python Package Index (PyPI) facilitates
   106	one of the largest software ecosystems, hosting more than 450k projects and their 9M Python releases. The extensive software reuse in PyPI emphasizes the importance of studying and addressing
   107	dependency bloat in this ecosystem. Our study seeks answers to the following research questions.
   108	
   109	
   110	**RQ1** **(Prevalence) How prevalent is bloated dependency code in the PyPI ecosystem?** What
   111	are the qualitative characteristics of bloated dependency code at different granularities (i.e.,
   112	package, file, method)? (Section 3.1)
   113	**RQ2** **(Security) What is the relation between bloated dependency code and software vul-**
   114	**nerabilities?** Do vulnerabilities reside in bloated code regions? (Section 3.2)
   115	**RQ3** **(Causes) What are the main causes of bloated PyPI dependencies?** What is the frequency
   116	of these causes? (Section 3.3)
   117	**RQ4** **(Developer perception) To what extent are developers willing to remove bloated PyPI**
   118	**dependencies?** Does the cause of bloated code affect developer decision and responsiveness?
   119	(Section 3.4)
   120	
   121	
   122	Proc. ACM Softw. Eng., Vol. 1, No. FSE, Article 114. Publication date: July 2024.
   123	
   124	
   125	Bloat beneath Python’s Scales: A Fine-Grained Inter-Project Dependency Analysis 114:3
   126	
   127	
   128	To answer these questions, we design and implement a large scale, fine-grained analysis on
   129	popular Python projects. We select 1,302 GitHub Python projects from a well-established dataset [Alfadel M 2020]. For each project, we build the fine-grained project dependency graph (FPDG), which
   130	captures the entire dependency network of a project at the method level. To do so, we fetch the
   131	source code of every project along with the source code of its direct and transitive dependencies.
   132	Following the approach of Keshani [2021] for practical and large-scale analyses, we then employ
   133	PyCG [Salis et al . 2021], the state-of-the-art static analyzer for Python, to construct the (partial) call
   134	graph of every Python project and dependency. Through a process called stitching, we merge all
   135	partial call graphs and derive the final FPDG. Stitching essentially connects an external method in a
   136	partial call graph (client code) with its counterpart found in the dependent call graph (library code).
   137	Running standard reachability analyses on the resulting FPDGs allows the assessment of bloated
   138	dependency code. We evaluate the identified bloated code in terms of several aspects, including its
   139	prevalence, its relation to software vulnerabilities, its main causes, and developer perception.
   140	**Contributions:** Our work makes the following contributions.
   141	
   142	
   143	  We conduct the first large-scale analysis for constructing fine-grained dependency networks
   144	in the PyPI ecosystem. We provide a corresponding reference dataset containing dependency
   145	networks that come from 1,302 Python projects and 3,232 PyPI releases (Section 2).
   146	
   147	  - We quantify bloat in the PyPI ecosystem, and provide a thorough assessment of its prevalence,
   148	its security impact, its main causes, and developer perception (Section 3).
   149	
   150	  We enumerate the implications of our findings, and discuss potential future directions in
   151	software dependency management (Section 4).
   152	
   153	
   154	**Summary of findings:** We find that the PyPI ecosystem exhibits severe dependency underutilization (RQ1). On average, a Python project consists of ten bloated dependencies. Bloated
   155	dependency code becomes more prevalent when measuring bloat at the level of files or methods.
   156	In particular, 87% of the dependency source files and 95% of the external methods in a Python
   157	project are bloated. Further, our fine-grained analysis highlights the security concerns related to
   158	bloated dependency code (RQ2): We identify several vulnerabilities in bloated areas of utilized
   159	packages (15% of the total defects in PyPI). Currently there are no attack techniques to directly
   160	exploit such defects (i.e., application vulnerabilities have to reside in reachable parts of the code
   161	to be exploited [Azad et al . 2023]). However, vulnerabilities that exist in bloated code can act as
   162	“time-bombs" which in turn can get activated after a slight code change. Furthermore, bloated
   163	dependencies are mainly introduced by pervasive changes in developers’ code base (e.g., feature
   164	removals) (RQ3). Finally, developers are willing to remove bloated dependencies, especially when
   165	they are aware of their main causes (RQ4).
   166	**Implications:** Through our analysis, we directly addressed bloated dependencies by submitting
   167	pull requests, targeting 42 cases across 36 Python projects. Our efforts have already led to the
   168	successful debloating of 35 dependencies in 30 projects, underlining the impact of our work. We
   169	believe that our study can help researchers and practitioners to build appropriate linters and
   170	automated refactoring tools to detect and eliminate bloated dependency code in Python projects.
   171	
   172	
   173	**2** **METHODOLOGY**
   174	
   175	Figure 1 summarizes our approach for studying bloated code within the PyPI ecosystem. As a
   176	starting point ( 1○ ), we select a set of popular and well-established Python projects based on a
   177	specific criteria (Section 2.2). Then, for each selected project, we resolve its direct and transitive
   178	dependencies (Section 2.2), and download the source code of each dependency for further analysis.
   179	The data analysis step (  - 2 ) begins with the construction of the partial (stand-alone) call graph of
   180	every individual project and dependency using the state-of-the-art static analysis tool PyCG [Salis
