   223	appear in the call graphs of the callee dependencies. We call the resulting (stitched) call graph,
   224	the fine-grained project dependency graph (FPDG) of a project. The FPDG captures the caller-callee
   225	relationships within a project’s entire dependency tree. Following the construction of the FPDG, we
   226	perform a reachability analysis to identify the packages, files, or methods that are accessible within
   227	each application. All unreachable methods are marked as bloated, and serve the basis for detecting
   228	bloated files and bloated dependencies according to a set of formal definitions (Section 2.1).
   229	In addition to the quantitative analysis, we also perform a qualitative analysis ( 3○ ) on our bloated
   230	code results. The goal of our qualitative analysis is twofold. First, we identify the root causes of code
   231	bloat. Second, we map previously-reported Python vulnerabilities taken from GitHub’s advisory
   232	database [gad 2023] to the individual methods these vulnerabilities come from. This allows us to
   233	determine whether these vulnerabilities are associated with bloated dependency code.
