     1	# Installation Guide
     2	This file  includes instructions and documentation for setting up the necessary environment in order run our code.
     3	Note that the following instructions may not work on any machine, as they have only been tested on Ubuntu and Debian, however we recommend using Docker (see [Requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md#)).
     4	If you encounter any issues, please contact us.
     5	
     6	## Getting Started
     7	
     8	Before we begin, make sure that your machine  meets the specifications listed in the  [Requirements file](REQUIREMENTS.md).
     9	
    10	First, obtain the artifact by cloning the repository and navigating to the artifact's root directory:
    11	
    12	```bash
    13	   git clone https://github.com/gdrosos/bloat-study-artifact ~/bloat-study-artifact
    14	   cd ~/bloat-study-artifact
    15	```
    16	To replicate the environment needed to run our analyses and scripts, you can choose between setting up a virtual environment directly on Ubuntu/Debian or using Docker, which is recommended.
    17	## Option1: Ubuntu/Debian Installation
    18	
    19	You need to install some packages through  `apt`  to run the
    20	experiments of this artifact.
    21	First, install git, python, pip, wget, unzip and python3-venv:
    22	
    23	```bash
    24	sudo apt update
    25	sudo apt install git python3 python3-pip  wget unzip python3-venv
    26	```
    27	**Important Note**
    28	For convenience, throughout the documentation and scripts, we use the standard python command instead of python3. To ensure compatibility, please create a symbolic link to point python to python3 by running the following command:
    29	```bash
    30	ln -s /usr/bin/python3 /usr/bin/python
    31	```
    32	
    33	You also need to install some Python packages.
    34	In a Python `virtualenv` run the following:
    35	```bash
    36	python -m venv .env
    37	source .env/bin/activate
    38	pip3 install -r requirements.txt
    39	```
    40	
    41	## Option2: Docker Image Installation (Recommended)
    42	
    43	Use this option if you prefer a containerized environment or are not using an Ubuntu/Debian operating system.
    44	We provide a `Dockerfile` to build an image that contains:
    45	
    46	* The necessary `apt` packages (e.g., `git`, `python3`, `pip`, `sudo`) for running the
    47	  our experiments.
    48	* The necessary Python packages (declared in the `requirements.txt` file).
    49	* A user named `user` with `sudo` privileges.
    50	
    51	To build the Docker image named `bloat-study-artifact` from source,
    52	run the following command (estimated running time: ~7 minutes):
    53	
    54	```bash
    55	docker build -t bloat-study-artifact .
    56	```
    57	Then, you can run  the docker container by executing the following command:
    58	
    59	```bash
    60	docker run -it --rm \
    61	    -v $(pwd)/scripts:/home/user/scripts \
    62	    -v $(pwd)/data:/home/user/data \
    63	    -v $(pwd)/figures:/home/user/figures \
    64	    bloat-study-artifact /bin/bash
    65	```
    66	After executing the command, you will be able to enter the home directory
    67	(i.e., `/home/user`). This directory contains:
    68	1) the scripts for reproducing the results of the paper (see `scripts/`),
    69	2)  the data of our bloat study (see `data/`),
    70	3) a dedicated directory for storing the generated figures (see `figures/`),
    71	
    72	
    73	This setup uses volume mounting (-v) to ensure that scripts, data, and figures directories are persisted outside of the container for ease of access and modification on your local machine (e.g. they will not be lost upon the container's exit).
    74	
    75	# Important Note
    76	In order to run some parts of our methodology, you will need a Github access token (see [here](https://docs.github.com/en/github/authenticating-to-github/creating-a-personal-access-token)).
    77	Once you obtain it,
    78	please assign it to a shell variable named `GH_TOKEN`.
    79	
    80	```bash
    81	export GH_TOKEN=<your Github access token>
    82	```
